Algebra and Trigonometry 2e — Abramson and contributors (@ql-source-algebra: 0, is #book, #part-of @ql-sources) { r_7FKH7S2VNZT97STHJ10KB186YX
Jay Abramson and contributing authors. Algebra and Trigonometry 2e. OpenStax, Rice University, 2021. English.

[Book and PDF access](https://openstax.org/details/books/algebra-and-trigonometry-2e) · [Preface, contributors, and scope](https://openstax.org/books/algebra-and-trigonometry-2e/pages/preface).

For the technical reading foundation, study chapters 1–4, 6–7, sections 8.1–8.3, sections 10.1–10.2 and 10.5–10.8, and chapter 11's linear systems. Follow the topic titles if section numbering changes. These selections cover algebra, functions, logarithms, angles, periodicity, complex numbers, and vectors. Chapter 5 is a useful full continuation for polynomial and rational models; chapter 12 extends geometric work. The whole book is a substantial precalculus continuation and is assigned at the later study stage when those deeper mathematical tools are wanted.

The publisher currently labels its web edition CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, with separate component and reuse notices. The particular downloaded edition's notice governs its reuse. Link to and consult the authorized edition; nothing is bundled here. Metadata and current preface checked on 28 October 2026.

**Return:** [Quantitative literacy sources — books, standards, and articles](../Sources.lingua#r_7MMXWNA1B4YGA271KMHPPJEK9Q).

**Mathematics-core inspection — 31 October 2026:** selected publisher passages in [1.1](https://openstax.org/books/algebra-and-trigonometry-2e/pages/1-1-real-numbers-algebra-essentials), [3.1](https://openstax.org/books/algebra-and-trigonometry-2e/pages/3-1-functions-and-function-notation), [3.2](https://openstax.org/books/algebra-and-trigonometry-2e/pages/3-2-domain-and-range) and [3.7](https://openstax.org/books/algebra-and-trigonometry-2e/pages/3-7-inverse-functions) were checked for number domains, functions and inverse-domain restrictions. This does not claim inspection of every assigned chapter or exercise. Original trail examples use separate hypothetical values and independently checked arithmetic.


**Second mathematics-block inspection — 31 October 2026:** the publisher's [10.8 vector discussion](https://openstax.org/books/algebra-and-trigonometry-2e/pages/10-8-vectors), [11.1 two-variable systems](https://openstax.org/books/algebra-and-trigonometry-2e/pages/11-1-systems-of-linear-equations-two-variables), [11.5 matrix operations](https://openstax.org/books/algebra-and-trigonometry-2e/pages/11-5-matrices-and-matrix-operations), and [11.6 Gaussian elimination](https://openstax.org/books/algebra-and-trigonometry-2e/pages/11-6-solving-systems-with-gaussian-elimination) were inspected for components/dot products, solution classification, array operations and elimination. The lessons assign progressive reading and exercises from those sections; this is not an audit of every exercise. Our row-operation explanation explicitly requires a nonzero row multiplier, distinguishes echelon from reduced echelon form, and permits missing pivots. The new geometric and affine examples are original. Existing wider readings and rights notices remain applicable.


**Fourth mathematics-core inspection — 31 October 2026:** freshly inspected selected definitions, identities and scope in [7.3 unit circle](https://openstax.org/books/algebra-and-trigonometry-2e/pages/7-3-unit-circle), [8.1 periodic graphs](https://openstax.org/books/algebra-and-trigonometry-2e/pages/8-1-graphs-of-the-sine-and-cosine-functions), [8.3 inverse domains](https://openstax.org/books/algebra-and-trigonometry-2e/pages/8-3-inverse-trigonometric-functions), [9.2 addition identities](https://openstax.org/books/algebra-and-trigonometry-2e/pages/9-2-sum-and-difference-identities), [2.4 complex arithmetic](https://openstax.org/books/algebra-and-trigonometry-2e/pages/2-4-complex-numbers) and [10.5 polar powers/roots](https://openstax.org/books/algebra-and-trigonometry-2e/pages/10-5-polar-form-of-complex-numbers). Add these sections to the progressive common reading. The new explanations define complex multiplication through pairs, retain inverse-domain and argument choices, and distinguish a root equation from a selected branch. Current attribution/CC BY-NC-SA and additional-use notices were rechecked; this is bounded passage inspection, not a full chapter/exercise audit. The rotation, initial-state and sample-ambiguity investigations are original, and source media stay external.


**Fifth mathematics-core inspection — 31 October 2026:** freshly inspected the official section 11.6 opening text on augmented matrices, equation interpretation and row operations. The common rectangular-system lesson explicitly requires nonzero row scaling, allows skipped pivot columns and distinguishes echelon from reduced echelon form; the opening square full-rank presentation is not used as a universal definition. Full worked source examples and exercises remain assigned rather than claimed performed. Earlier edition, license/component notices and inspections remain; the new finite-system investigation and programs are original, with source media external.

